issues.body:
### What happened?

If you use:

{
  "mcpServers": {
    "example": {"url": "${MY_VAR}"}
  }
}

Doesn't resolve the transport correctly. If I use "http://${MY_VAR_WITHOUT_SCHEMA}" then it works.

The url should be resolved with the same logic used for headers and env values.

I believe mcp-servers.ts in validateMcpServerConfig() should use the url after environment variable expansion. Same issue in runtime.ts in createDefaultTransport(). Same issue in McpServerConnection.oauthUrl.

### Steps to reproduce

use:

{
  "mcpServers": {
    "example": {"url": "${MY_VAR}"}
  }
}

In mcp.json

### Expected behavior

This mcp.json:

{
  "mcpServers": {
    "example": {"url": "${MY_VAR}"}
  }
}

Should be able to connect as streamable-http.

### Version

1.0.4

### What happened?

`NodeExecutionEnv.exec()` sometimes settles with exit code 0 and none of the command's output, so the bash tool returns an empty result for something like `echo hi`. We see it in a host that is busy right after reopening a Harness (recovered tasks, start-up work), about 1 run in 80 under CPU load, with `@earendil-works/pi-durable` 1.5.2 on macOS and Node 26.

### Cause

In `waitForChildProcess` (`packages/durable/src/env/node.ts`), `onExit` arms the `EXIT_STDIO_GRACE_MS` (100 ms) timer, and when it fires without new data, `finalize()` destroys stdout and stderr. Each turn of the event loop runs timers before the poll phase that reads pipes. So if the loop is blocked for more than 100 ms after `exit` is handled and before the pipe is read, the next turn fires the timer first, and `finalize()` destroys the streams with the output still unread in the pipe.

With logging added to a failing run: `exit 0` at t, no `data`, the grace timer at t+207 ms with stdout not ended, and the result's output `""`.

This is related to #5303 / #5753 but is a different case: there the output was written more than 100 ms after exit; here it was written in time and is only read late.

### Repro

```ts
// Each command exits at once and leaves a child that writes 50 ms later; the loop is then busy for 150 ms after the exit.
import childProcess from "node:child_process";
import { syncBuiltinESMExports } from "node:module";
const realSpawn = childProcess.spawn;
(childProcess as any).spawn = (...args: any[]) => {
	const child = (realSpawn as any)(...args);
	const emit = child.emit.bind(child);
	child.emit = (event: string, ...rest: unknown[]) => {
		const result = emit(event, ...rest);
		if (event === "exit") { const until = Date.now() + 150; while (Date.now() < until) {} }
		return result;
	};
	return child;
};
syncBuiltinESMExports();
const { NodeExecutionEnv } = await import("@earendil-works/pi-durable/env/node");
const env = new NodeExecutionEnv({ cwd: process.cwd() });
let lost = 0;
for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
	let out = "";
	await env.exec("(sleep 0.05; echo late) & echo early", { onOutput: (t: string) => void (out += t) }, {} as any);
	if (!out.includes("late")) lost++;
}
console.log(`${lost}/10 lost`); // 1.5.2: 10/10 lost
process.exit(0);
```

### Suggested fix

Wait one `setImmediate` when the timer fires, so the poll phase reads what is already in the pipe first; `onData` then arms a new timer:

```ts
const armIdleTimer = (): void => {
	if (postExitTimer) clearTimeout(postExitTimer);
	const timer = setTimeout(() => {
		setImmediate(() => {
			if (settled || postExitTimer !== timer) return;
			if (spillIsDraining()) armIdleTimer();
			else finalize();
		});
	}, EXIT_STDIO_GRACE_MS);
	postExitTimer = timer;
};
```

With this the repro loses 0/10, plain commands are unchanged, and `sleep 5 & echo held` (a background process holding stdout) still settles in about 190 ms. We are carrying this as a local patch of `dist/env/node.js` for now.

## Problem

Two bugs affecting providers that return non-standard streaming responses:

### 1. Array content (`[object Object],[object Object]`)

Some Databricks models (Qwen3, gpt-oss reasoning models) return `choice.delta.content` as a **typed array** when tools are present:

```json
[{"type": "reasoning", "summary": [...]}, {"type": "text", "text": "Hello!"}]
```

The current code does:
```typescript
block.text += choice.delta.content; // array + string = "[object Object],[object Object]"
```

This produces garbled output instead of the actual response text.

### 2. Missing `finish_reason` (`Stream ended without finish_reason`)

Some providers (Databricks Kimi `kimi-k2-7-code`, `inkling`) never set `finish_reason` in any streaming chunk. The stream completes successfully with `[DONE]` but no chunk ever has `finish_reason`, causing Pi to throw an error even though the response content was received.

## Proposed Fix

**Fix 1** — extract only `type: "text"` blocks from array content:

```typescript
const rawContent = choice.delta.content;
const textContent = Array.isArray(rawContent)
    ? (rawContent as Array<{ type?: string; text?: string }>)
        .filter((b) => b?.type === "text")
        .map((b) => b?.text ?? "")
        .join("")
    : rawContent;
if (textContent.length > 0) {
    const block = ensureTextBlock();
    block.text += textContent;
    stream.push({ type: "text_delta", contentIndex: getContentIndex(block), delta: textContent, partial: output });
}
```

**Fix 2** — add `supportsFinishReason?: boolean` to `OpenAICompletionsCompat` (default `true`):

```typescript
// in types.ts
supportsFinishReason?: boolean;

// in openai-completions.ts
if (!hasFinishReason && compat.supportsFinishReason !== false) {
    throw new Error("Stream ended without finish_reason");
}
```

A ready-to-merge PR with both fixes is at: https://github.com/TomeHirata/pi/tree/fix/array-content-and-finish-reason

**What happened?**

A change to the selected tool set made while a run is in progress moves the head of the
system prompt, so the run's next request re-bills from that point. The head is where the
`- <tool>: <snippet>` bullets are rendered, and it re-derives the selection from the live
set every turn, so adding or removing one selected tool rewrites it.

Measured on one session (72 responses, provider `deepseek`): five requests followed such a
change, each exactly one tool bullet (266 characters); a sixth head move came from the
injected path described in the comment below. Two of the six re-billed the whole prompt —
156,362 and 192,928 input tokens, with only 1,664 and 128 tokens cached. The other four
re-billed 2.7–17.8% of the prompt.

**Steps to reproduce**

Validated with `pi -ne`, so no ambient extensions were loaded. One small extension is
enough — it registers a tool with a `promptSnippet`, selects it for the run, and removes it
from the live set during the first tool call:

```ts
// repro.ts — pi --mode rpc -ne -e ./capture.ts -e ./repro.ts
import { Type } from "typebox";

export default function (pi: any) {
  const TOOL = "probe_enable";
  pi.registerTool({
    name: TOOL,
    description: "Scratch tool that exists so the system prompt can carry a bullet for it.",
    parameters: Type.Object({}, { additionalProperties: false }),
    promptSnippet: `${TOOL} is installed. Call it to enable the scratch tools.`,
    async execute() { return { content: [{ type: "text", text: "ok" }] }; },
  });

  let removed = false;
  pi.on("tool_call", () => {
    if (removed) return undefined;
    removed = true;
    pi.setActiveTools(pi.getActiveTools().filter((n) => n !== TOOL));
    return undefined;
  });

  pi.on("before_agent_start", (event) => {
    const active = pi.getActiveTools();
    if (!active.includes(TOOL)) pi.setActiveTools([...active, TOOL]);
    const selected = event.systemPromptOptions?.selectedTools;
    if (Array.isArray(selected) && !selected.includes(TOOL)) selected.push(TOOL);
    return undefined;
  });
}
```

Capture what the provider is sent (wrap `globalThis.fetch` and write `init.body` to a
file), then send one prompt that makes the model call any tool:

- before the tool call: system prompt 13,796 chars, `- probe_enable: …` present;
- after it: 13,716 chars, and the **only** difference in the system prompt is that bullet
  line (79 chars + newline);
- later requests stay there, so it is billed once — but the whole head moved.

**Expected behavior**

A tool-set change made during a run should not rewrite the head of a prompt that has
already been sent, or — if it must — the cost should not be the conversation behind it.
Rendering the bullets from the same set the request will carry would close the
prompt/payload half; applying selection changes at a run boundary would close the rest.
Happy to prepare a PR if that is the direction you prefer.

**Version**

0.87.1 (Linux; provider `deepseek`, model `deepseek-flash`, openai-completions).

## Summary

The exec approval card can tell the operator that a command **contains an inline eval**, but not that the directory it is about to `rm -rf` is a **mount point**. Those two facts have very different blast radii, and only the syntactic one reaches the human who clicks Allow.

In `2026.8.1-beta.2` the card is built by `buildExecApprovalPendingReplyPayload` (`src/gateway/…/exec-approval-reply.ts`, shipped as `dist/exec-approval-reply-DNHX4phi.js`):

```js
const lines = [];
const warningText = params.warningText?.trim();
if (warningText) lines.push(warningText);
lines.push("Approval required.");
…
lines.push("Pending command:");
lines.push(formatFencedCodeBlock(params.command, "sh"));
…
const info = [];
info.push(`Host: ${params.host}`);
if (params.nodeId) info.push(`Node: ${params.nodeId}`);
if (params.cwd) info.push(`CWD: ${formatApprovalDisplayPath(params.cwd)}`);
```

Two things follow from that shape:

1. **The only path the card renders is `cwd`**, and the only transform applied to it is `formatApprovalDisplayPath` (`src/infra/approval-display-paths.ts`), which compacts `$HOME` and nothing else. The path a destructive command *targets* is visible only inside the raw command text.
2. **The risk channel already exists and is purely syntactic.** `warningText` is fed from `CommandExplanationSummary { riskKinds, warningLines }` (`src/infra/command-analysis/explain.ts`), and every producer works on the parsed command alone:

```js
function summarizeCommandSegmentsForDisplay(segments) {
	const riskKinds = [];
	const warningLines = [];
	const inlineEval = detectInlineEvalInSegments(segments);
	if (inlineEval) {
		riskKinds.push("inline-eval");
		warningLines.push(`Contains inline-eval: ${inlineEval.normalizedExecutable} ${inlineEval.flag}`);
	}
	for (const segment of segments) {
		const effectiveArgv = segment.resolution?.effectiveArgv ?? segment.argv;
		for (const hit of detectCommandCarrierArgv(effectiveArgv)) {
			riskKinds.push("command-carrier");
			warningLines.push(hit.flag ? `Contains command-carrier: ${hit.command} ${hit.flag}` : `Contains command-carrier: ${hit.command}`);
		}
	}
	…
}
```

`inline-eval`, `command-carrier`, `source`, `function-definition` — all decidable from the string. Nothing consults the filesystem state of the operand, which is where the difference between "deleting a directory" and "deleting the contents underneath somebody's mount" lives.

## What Problem This Solves

`rm -rf /some/path` where `/some/path` is a mount point does **not** mean what the approver reads it to mean. It empties the mounted filesystem, and if the mount is later lost the kernel hands back the empty underlying directory — so the program that owned it silently re-initializes on the wrong device, with no error and no log line.

That is not hypothetical; it is what it cost us on 2026-08-07:

- An agent hit repeated `blob not found` errors from `containerd` and asked to run `sudo rm -rf /var/lib/containerd && systemctl restart docker` on a Linux container host. The justification was plausible, so the operator **approved it** — the approval flow worked exactly as designed.
- `/var/lib/containerd` was the target of a bind/dataset mount. The `rm -rf` removed the store *under* the mount.
- The mount was subsequently absent from `/proc/mounts`, `containerd` initialized a **fresh image store on the root filesystem**, and Docker kept working. Zero errors.
- **Five hours** of builds and pulls went to the wrong filesystem (11 GB on a root subvolume that had already filled once that week for an unrelated reason), while 12.4 GB of the real store sat orphaned on the dedicated dataset.
- Recovery was a maintenance window: stop the container, `rsync` the store back onto the dataset, verify blob counts match (353 = 353), restart, re-verify 18/18 services.

Nothing in that chain was a framework bug. What was missing was one sentence in front of the person deciding: *this path is a mount point.* Blocking the command is the wrong fix — the operator would have approved a blocked command too, and it would have been correct to. The gap is **information at the moment of the decision**, which is precisely what `warningLines` is for.

## Proposal

Add a filesystem-state risk to command analysis, rendered through the existing `warningLines` channel:

- **New risk kind**, e.g. `mount-point-target`, producing `Contains mount-point-target: /var/lib/containerd` (optionally naming the source device).
- **Trigger:** a destructive operand (`rm`, `mv` source, `mkfs`, `dd of=`, `chown -R`/`chmod -R` are the obvious candidates; `rm` alone would already earn its keep) whose **resolved path is itself a mount point** on the host that will execute the command.
- **Cheapest correct test, Linux:** `stat` the path and its parent and compare `st_dev`; different device = mount point. One `stat` pair per operand, no `/proc/mounts` parsing, no distro or hypervisor knowledge. `/proc/mounts` only if you want to name the source device in the message.
- **Deliberately generic.** Our case was Proxmox (`mp2` in `pct config`) but Proxmox has no business in the core — "the target path is a mount point" is the portable statement, and `mp` is just how one hypervisor spells it. The same annotation covers NFS mounts, `docker`/`podman` volumes, LUKS mounts, and USB media.

**The honest design cost, stated up front:** the check must run **where the command will execute**. `resolveCommandAnalysisSummaryForDisplay` currently runs gateway-side for both hosts, and for `host: "node"` the operand path exists on the node, not on the gateway — so a naive `stat` in the current call site would be *wrong for the case that matters most* (remote exec), and answering "not a mount point" for a path that does not exist locally is worse than silence. Either the node returns the annotation with its approval request, or the check is scoped to `host: "gateway"`/`"sandbox"` in a first pass and documented as such.

A weaker variant that needs no new plumbing at all: render the **resolved absolute target path** in the info block next to `CWD:`, so at minimum the approver sees what the shell will see after expansion.

## Evidence

- Bundle read at `2026.8.1-beta.2`: `dist/exec-approval-reply-DNHX4phi.js` (card assembly, quoted above), `dist/approval-display-paths-DlQSsCnq.js` (`formatApprovalDisplayPath` — `$HOME` compaction only, nothing else), `dist/exec-approval-DvFW-diU.js` (`summarizeCommandExplanation`, `summarizeCommandSegmentsForDisplay`, `resolveCommandAnalysisSummaryForDisplay`), `dist/exec-approvals-core-ByvfWxmW.d.ts` (`CommandExplanationSummary { commandCount, nestedCommandCount, riskKinds, warningLines }`).
- Incident of 2026-08-07 summarized above: approval granted on a `rm -rf` whose operand was a mount point; 5 h of writes to the wrong filesystem; 11 GB misplaced, 12.4 GB orphaned; recovery required a service window and a blob-count verification (353 = 353) before anything was deleted.
- We looked for an existing report first: `approval` + `mount` searches on this repo return the approval-UI issues (#115876, #94599, #97043, #98576, #113262), none of which touch the filesystem state of the operand.

## Environment

- `openclaw@2026.8.1-beta.2`, installed with `npm i -g` under `~/.local/lib/node_modules`.
- Linux (Proxmox host, Debian LXC guests), Node v22.23.1.
- Exec runs both `host: "gateway"` and `host: "node"`; the incident command ran through an agent's exec with approvals on (`ask: on-miss`).
- Local customizations disclosed: we run in-house plugins (a context engine and a memory MCP) and carry local dist patches for unrelated defects. None of them touch exec approvals or command analysis, and the card text quoted above is stock.

## Impact and severity

Enhancement, not a regression. Severity is in the failure mode it fails to prevent: an approval that is **granted on an incomplete reading of the command**, whose consequence is silent (no error, no log, the service keeps serving) and whose time-to-notice is measured in hours. For anyone running agents against hosts with mounted storage — containers, NAS mounts, external volumes — the annotation is one line of text standing between a routine cleanup and a filesystem that quietly moved.

> **Form note (edited 2026-10-08):** this is a feature request that was filed in bug shape. The sections below add what `feature_request.yml` asks for (alternatives, implementation intent) without changing the evidence above.

## Model / provider routing chain

**Unknown** — not recorded for the 2026-08-07 incident. The approval card is built from command analysis, independent of the model that proposed the command.

## Alternatives considered

1. **Block destructive commands on mount points.** Rejected (see *What Problem This Solves*): the operator would have approved it anyway, correctly; the gap is information, not permission.
2. **Parse `/proc/mounts`** instead of the `st_dev` comparison: heavier, and only needed to name the source device.
3. **Gateway-side check only, as a first pass** (`host: "gateway"`/`"sandbox"`), documented as such, with the node returning its own annotation later — see the design-cost note under *Proposal*.
4. **Do nothing in core; rely on operator memory of the host layout.** That is what failed on 2026-08-07.

## Implementation intent

No PR from us unless a maintainer asks for one; the design choice in point 3 (where the check runs for `host: "node"`) needs a maintainer call first.

## Summary
A provider-generated, multi-segment MP3 was reported to lose most of its decoded audio when converted to Opus-in-CAF with the existing macOS recipe, despite a successful exit status. First-segment metadata handling by AudioToolbox is suspected, not confirmed. The public generated-tone fixture does **not** reproduce the clipping. This is an observed-bug report with an unavailable private triggering sample, not a verified public regression or a proven fix.

## Environment and version context
- Reported runtime: OpenClaw 2026.10.10; macOS 27.0.1 (26A434), arm64.
- The published `openclaw@2026.11.3` npm package was inspected on 2026-11-11. Its media-services `transcodeAudioBuffer` path is unchanged: the CAF recipe still invokes `/usr/bin/afconvert -f caff -d opus@24000 -c 1` directly. The issue is therefore believed present in the latest stable release inspected, but clipping was not runtime-tested on 2026.11.3.
- Candidate native-source base: public commit `b029a471858e63304a3c6e6e1b6d698fbf2fdc65` in https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw. This snapshot and the runtime releases are different artifacts.

## Reported observation and baseline measurement
The reported provider sample decoded to **60.026 seconds** before conversion and **4.210 seconds** afterward, while afconvert returned success. The original recording is not attached, and these historical measurements cannot be independently authenticated from this issue.

For a multi-segment MP3 available to the tester, the baseline can be measured without applying the candidate patch:

```sh
afconvert -f caff -d opus@24000 -c 1 input.mp3 output.caf
ffmpeg -nostdin -hide_banner -loglevel error -i input.mp3 -ar 24000 -ac 1 -f s16le source.pcm
ffmpeg -nostdin -hide_banner -loglevel error -i output.caf -ar 24000 -ac 1 -f s16le target.pcm
wc -c source.pcm target.pcm
```

Use fresh output filenames. Divide each PCM byte count by 48,000 for decoded seconds (mono, 24 kHz, signed 16-bit). Do not rely on MP3 header duration. These commands measure a supplied sample; they do not create a public triggering fixture.

## Synthetic compatibility results—not a reproducer
A generated-tone scaffold creates three independently encoded four-second tones, byte-concatenates fifteen segments, and compares the original recipe with the candidate. Repeated ID3 tags are suppressed in this control fixture. Recorded decoded durations were:

| Measurement | Seconds |
| --- | ---: |
| Source MP3 | 61.075375 |
| Direct afconvert baseline | 61.110208 |
| Candidate output | 61.087708 |

The baseline preserves all segments on this fixture. These measurements support compatibility on that fixture only; they do not demonstrate correction of the reported clipping. Injected clipped target frames separately exercise mismatch rejection, not native failing-before/passing-after behavior. Exact FFmpeg version information is not available in this report.

A useful next experiment would retain default Xing/Info and ID3 metadata per segment, vary sample rate and bitrate, and compare decoded frame counts and segment frequencies against the metadata-suppressed control. This is proposed work, not a verified reproducer; no variant is claimed to fail.

## Expected and actual behavior
Expected: preserve all decoded audio when generating CAF. If a format is genuinely required and conversion fails or loses substantial audio, retain visible text and omit generated audio.

Actual: the reported sample was truncated despite successful conversion and a nonempty CAF. The suspected decoder explanation remains unproven. Separately, the synthesis failure path can continue persisting original provider bytes after a conversion failure, potentially exposing those bytes to another lossy conversion.

## Candidate approach
- For MP3 → CAF, decode via FFmpeg to mono 24 kHz PCM, wrap as WAV, then use the existing afconvert Opus/CAF recipe. Compare decoded source/final frame counts within max(250 ms, 1%). Frame counts detect duration loss, not semantic speech equivalence.
- Reject MP3 inputs above 50 MiB and empty/overlong decoded audio (600-second limit). Share a 30-second subprocess deadline, respecting shorter explicit timeouts. Filesystem work and cleanup can extend total elapsed time; size sentinels are not hard filesystem quotas.
- Return failed synthesis before audio persistence when preferred-format conversion fails or throws. Existing ordinary automatic-payload handling then preserves visible text and non-audio fields.

**Policy question:** preferred is not necessarily required. The synthesis change below makes every unmet preferred format fatal across platforms/formats, including previously intentional unsupported-platform/no-recipe skips. Maintainer agreement on that behavior is required; otherwise scope failure handling to genuinely required conversion or propose it separately.

A candidate patch and a standalone regression harness are available; I can open a PR on request. The two production diffs are included below. The harness is not included inline.

## Evidence limits and merge gates
- A shareable triggering fixture and verified native failing-before/passing-after regression are still needed before claiming this fixes the original clipping.
- Recorded standalone results comprise 18 passing cases plus two measurement rows. They use stripped native functions with real local codecs and mocked provider, persistence, temp/process adapters and payload dependencies—not the full rebuilt import graph.
- Native TypeScript typecheck, formatting/lint, package build, native unit/integration tests and affected-platform CI remain required. Added formatting needs upstream cleanup. There was no full native build, transport/queue-recovery test, live delivery or deployment acceptance.
- Add native coverage for helper output overflow, timeout/cancellation/reaping, cleanup failures, 600-second and duration-tolerance boundaries, empty/overlong final output, and explicit-speech fallback. Strengthen the oversized-input test to count workspace calls; the same-container test checks dispatch with MP3 bytes labeled CAF, not CAF validity.
- Normalization adds FFmpeg where direct CAF conversion previously needed only afconvert. Missing FFmpeg intentionally becomes text-only. Repeated embedded ID3 metadata can overflow the 1024-byte stderr cap and safely reject legitimate output; realistic provider compatibility needs qualification.
- Cleanup can reject an otherwise successful conversion; caller rejection handling does not prove temporary files were removed. Persistent deletion failures and disk-full behavior need native tests. There is no global concurrency/resource reservation in this minimal candidate.
- Static user-facing errors reduce diagnostics; consider safe internal failure categories. Ordinary automatic visible-text fallback is tested; unrelated pre-existing media is not removed.
- Maintainers must agree that failed preferred-format conversion should disable audio across platforms, or narrow/separate that policy change.

