prs.body:
Related: #195030

## What Problem This Solves

Fixes: a Claude CLI conversation stalls silently, with no Claude process left, when the process owner that launched the warm Claude process is lost.

## User Impact

User impact: when the owner of a warm Claude process dies, the affected message now fails promptly with "Claude CLI process cleanup could not be confirmed; retry the message. If it fails again, restart the Gateway." Before, the message waited until its timeout without useful output.

This is a partial mitigation. The failed turn still reports unconfirmed cleanup. Current main removes a rejected retired cleanup entry after it settles ([#198224](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/198224)), so a later message can retry with a fresh process. Recovery of the reporter's original sequence is not proven; #195030 remains open.

## Why This Change Was Made

`waitForExit()` in `extensions/anthropic/cli-transport.ts` awaited only the child's exit event. A lost process owner reports an error (`service child cleanup identity lost`), never an exit event, and its `wait()` and `waitForExtinction()` both reject. The wait stayed pending forever. The live-session registry joins that wait before it lets a fresh turn start (`remove()` then `restartCliLiveSession()`), so each later turn sat behind it. That matches the issue: no `claude` process present, no `cli live session start`, `released=0`.

`waitForExit()` now also settles on the owner's own wait and extinction result. A rejection becomes an actionable error. A normal exit still waits for both, as before.

The abort-aware restart from the earlier commit (`1f79ff4de98`) is reverted. It bounded a wait the real hang does not depend on, and the transport already guarantees SIGTERM/SIGKILL escalation for a live process. Its test came out with it.

Provenance for the changed behavior:
- `waitForExit` waiting on `child.wait()` and `child.waitForExtinction()` came from #171016 (`321f4d3ded5`, stop Claude CLI sessions when the Gateway is killed). This change keeps both waits and stops the wait from depending on an event a lost owner never sends. The later registry repair in #198224 clears a settled cleanup rejection so a later turn can retry.
- The `exited` promise and the exit-error handling come from #138707 (`58d8871d18c`, direct CLI transport) and #165769 (`89792e5aeb9`). They are unchanged; `exited` is still awaited on the normal path.
- Reverting `1f79ff4de98` returns `restartCliLiveSession` to its state at base `90dfe90f3c8`.

## Evidence

Real Gateway built from this branch, isolated state (temporary `HOME`, `OPENCLAW_STATE_DIR`, own port), `claude-cli` runtime, and a small stand-in `claude` executable on `PATH` that speaks the stream-json protocol. Turns were driven with `openclaw agent`. The fault: after two normal turns, `SIGKILL` the process-owner helper that launched the warm Claude process.

Before (PR head `1f79ff4de98`): the next turns stall until their timeout, and the lane is reported stalled after 360 s.

```text
# fault: SIGKILL the process-owner helper (pid <helper>) of the warm fake claude (pid <claude>)
# fake claude still alive? no
$ openclaw agent --session-id proof-before --timeout 40 --message "T3 turn right after the fault"
CLI produced no output for 39s and was terminated. | cli_no_output_timeout      [elapsed 40s]
$ ... --message "T4 next turn"
Input <id> was withdrawn before its turn started and was not delivered.          [elapsed 41s]
$ ... --message "T5 turn after that"
Input <id> was withdrawn before its turn started and was not delivered.          [elapsed 41s]
gateway: lane task error: ... durationMs=360000 error="Reply operation stalled"
```

After (historical head `9ee02bd15fada32dae71c82f2ce9bc5d511a6440`, before the #198224 base update): the affected turns failed promptly with the error then in the branch. This trace was not rerun after the retry-first wording and registry update.

```text
$ openclaw agent --session-id proof-after --timeout 40 --message "T3 turn right after the fault"
Claude CLI process cleanup could not be confirmed; restart the Gateway before retrying. | service child cleanup identity lost: anchor channel closed without a matching closing receipt   [elapsed 1s]
$ ... --message "T4 next turn"      (same error, elapsed 1s)
$ ... --message "T5 turn after that" (same error, elapsed 1s)
```

The full harness (stand-in `claude` and run script) is in the details below. No screen recording was made; no recorder is installed on the test host.

<details><summary>Proof harness</summary>

`fake-claude-wrapper.js`:

```js
#!/usr/bin/env node
// Fake Claude Code for the wedge proof: speaks stream-json just enough for one warm session.
const fs = require("node:fs");
const readline = require("node:readline");
const args = process.argv.slice(2);
const dir = process.env.FAKE_CLAUDE_DIR || "/tmp";
const log = (m) => fs.appendFileSync(`${dir}/fake-claude.log`, `${new Date().toISOString()} pid=${process.pid} ${m}\n`);
if (args[0] === "auth") { console.log(JSON.stringify({ loggedIn: true, authMethod: "claude.ai", email: "user@example.invalid" })); process.exit(0); }
if (args.includes("--version") || args[0] === "-v") { console.log("2.1.318 (Claude Code)"); process.exit(0); }
const mode = () => { try { return fs.readFileSync(`${dir}/mode`, "utf8").trim(); } catch { return "normal"; } };
log(`start mode=${mode()}`);
if (mode() === "never-start") { setInterval(() => {}, 1000); } // hangs before reading stdin
else {
  const send = (v) => process.stdout.write(JSON.stringify(v) + "\n");
  let n = 0;
  process.on("SIGTERM", () => { log("SIGTERM"); if (mode() !== "ignore-term") process.exit(0); });
  const rl = readline.createInterface({ input: process.stdin });
  rl.on("line", (line) => {
    const m = JSON.parse(line);
    if (m.type === "control_request" && m.request.subtype === "initialize") {
      send({ type: "control_response", response: { subtype: "success", request_id: m.request_id, response: { commands: [], models: [] } } });
    } else if (m.type === "user") {
      n++; log(`turn ${n}`);
      send({ type: "system", subtype: "init", tools: [], session_id: "fake-session" });
      send({ type: "result", subtype: "success", is_error: false, result: `fake-reply-${n} pid=${process.pid}`, session_id: "fake-session" });
    }
  });
  rl.on("close", () => { log("stdin EOF"); process.exit(0); });
}
```

`run-proof.sh`:

```sh
#!/bin/bash
# usage: run-proof.sh <label> <session-id>   (gateway must already be running on :18991 with the fake claude on PATH)
P=<proof-dir>; . $P/env.sh; cd <home>/.t3/worktrees/openclaw-upstream/fix-claude-cli-live-session-wedge-v2
label=$1; sid=$2
ask(){ s=$(date +%s); out=$(timeout 150 node openclaw.mjs agent --agent main --session-id "$sid" --timeout "$2" --message "$1" 2>&1 | grep -v fnm_multishells | head -c 400); echo "\$ openclaw agent --session-id $sid --timeout $2 --message \"$1\""; echo "$out"; echo "[elapsed $(( $(date +%s)-s ))s]"; echo; }
echo "== $label: $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) =="
ask "T1 first turn" 60
ask "T2 second turn (warm session reused)" 60
CP=$(pgrep -f "^node $P/bin/claude --mcp-config" | tail -1); HP=$(ps -o ppid= -p "$CP" | tr -d ' ')
echo "# fault: SIGKILL the process-owner helper (pid $HP) of the warm fake claude (pid $CP)"; kill -9 "$HP"; sleep 2
echo "# fake claude still alive? $(kill -0 "$CP" 2>/dev/null && echo yes || echo no)"; echo
ask "T3 turn right after the fault" 40
ask "T4 next turn" 40
ask "T5 turn after that" 40
```

</details>

Regression test (red on the base transport, green here; re-verified independently): `cli-transport.close.test.ts` "rejects waitForExit when the process owner is lost and never reports an exit". On the base transport it times out; with the fix it passes. Adjacent suites pass: `execute-plugin.test.ts` and `cli-live-session-registry.test.ts` (81), `cli-runtime.test.ts` (70), `cli-transport.close.test.ts` (2). Test-line budget: the 38 new regression lines are allocated in `config/test-lines-allocations/claude-cli-owner-loss-exit-wait.txt` (the earlier registry test and its 37 lines were removed with the revert); `pnpm check:test-lines-ratchet` passes and the shared scalar is unchanged. `pnpm -s tsgo:core`, `run-tsgo.mjs -p test/tsconfig/tsconfig.extensions.test.json`, oxlint and `oxfmt --check` are clean.

Not covered:
- The owner loss is an injected fault. It is the only way I found to leave the exit wait pending, but I could not show it is what happened on the reporter's host.
- No automatic recovery. A follow-up could let a non-subreaper owner use `confirmExtinction()` (the node-host worker lifecycle already does) to prove the process group gone after loss. On Linux with the native owner it always reports unconfirmed, so it would not help the reporter's host.

## Maintainer correction and open follow-up

At `bdfb5a3cd7ca3566d577876b26477eb27fbc7ca4`, the cleanup error now tells the operator to retry the message first, with Gateway restart guidance only for repeated failure. The transport close test and live-session registry test passed locally (2 and 43 tests respectively); the exact-head hosted gates are pending.

Full recovery of the original reporter sequence remains unproven. Keep #195030 open for that separate investigation.

## What Problem This Solves

A healthy `claude-cli` run was killed by stuck-session recovery while it was making progress. The Gateway's view of the stream froze, `lastProgress` stayed pinned at `tool:Bash:started` and climbed to 920 s, and recovery fired `action=abort_embedded_run`; twelve in-process background agents died with the CLI process. The run itself was fine.

The parser has two cumulative per-turn odometers (8 MiB of retained characters, 20,000 frames). When either is spent, `main` latches a fatal parse error and the whole turn is reported as a format failure with empty text — including turns that had **already finished**, because the terminal `result` record is the last thing Claude writes. It also stops observing tool starts/results and attributed progress, which is what let stuck-run recovery misread a live turn as frozen.

Current `main` has only made that budget trip less often (#166127, #186085, #185404, and #184352's flat 64-char allowance for forwarded subagent records). It has not changed what happens when a budget **is** spent (`cli-output-stream.ts`: `accountClaudeJsonlLine` latches the error; `getOutput()` returns `{ text: "", errorText }` and never consults the `sawTerminalResult` it already tracks).

## Why This Change Was Made

Four changes, all inside the Claude stream-json path:

1. **A spent budget truncates instead of failing.** Assembly stops; the parser keeps watching for the terminal `result` and keeps emitting tool starts/results, attributed subagent progress and native compaction phases. A finished reply is delivered, truncation is reported separately (`getOutputTruncationText()`, logged as a warning by `execute-process.ts`), and a turn that never produces a terminal result still fails with the limit error. `maxPendingLineChars` stays fatal.
2. **Child-attributed stream events no longer leak into the parent reply.** The cumulative-snapshot branch of `parseClaudeCliStreamingDelta` already rejected `parent_tool_use_id` records; the partial-message branch did not, so child text deltas were appended to the parent reply. The guard is hoisted. Charging is unchanged: the record is still charged in full, as `cli-output-subagent-budget.test.ts` pins.
3. **Retention bounds the longer-lived parse needs** (`cli-output-tool-tracker.ts`, `execute-event-retention.ts`, `execute-messaging-evidence.ts`): caps on tracked tool ids, pending tool input and retained tool args. Keeping a spent budget alive means the consumers now run for as long as a stream can last, so every holder of a decoded tool argument needs a byte bound, not just a count.
4. **Settled delivery evidence is bounded too** (`execute-settled-evidence.ts`, new). The pending bound in (3) is refunded when a result arrives, so sends that each start and settle inside the 8 MiB allowance used to accumulate their full content in `messagingToolSentTexts` / `messagingToolSentMediaUrls` / `messagingToolSentTargets`, which capped entries (64) and not size. The settled holder now carries the same two-part bound as the pending one — a count cap plus one aggregate `MAX_SETTLED_MESSAGING_EVIDENCE_CHARS` (8 MiB) budget — and past the budget an entry is reduced by the same `reduceMessagingToolArgs` the pending side uses, keeping every routing fact and dropping only the content echo. Duplicate suppression survives the drop: the target key is a sha256 of the full send, and a dropped text or media url leaves its digest in a bounded set. `messagingToolSourceReplyPayloads` stays count-bounded on purpose — it is outbound content the host still has to deliver, not an echo of a delivery.

5. **Dropped-body dedupe evidence survives to the delivery consumer** (`execute-settled-evidence.ts`, `embedded-agent-messaging.types.ts`, `reply-payloads-dedupe.ts`). A reduced settled send carries `textComparisonDigest` — `sha256Hex(normalizeTextForComparison(text))` of the body it had to drop, 64 chars charged to the same reduced allowance — and `resolveMessagingToolPayloadDedupe` matches route-scoped payloads against those digests. Without it, past the settled budget a repeated body was invisible to route-scoped dedupe and the turn could deliver the same answer twice. Exact repeats only: the substring/ratio rule cannot be evaluated against a digest, so this is route-scoped target evidence, not a stand-in for the run's global sent-text set.
6. **No production measurement seams.** `getRetainedStateSizes` / `getRetainedMessagingSizes` and the counters that fed them are gone. They had no shipped-runner consumer; the caps are unchanged and are asserted where they are observable — emitted events, the exported evidence projection, and the proof harness's forced-collection heap delta.

**What this PR deliberately does not do:** it no longer exempts forwarded subagent traffic from the budget. Forwarded records count toward the 20,000-frame odometer and are charged by `main`'s `chargeClaudeStreamJsonLine` unchanged, so a discarded-record flood is still bounded.

## User Impact

A parent turn that finished is delivered even if its stream outgrew a budget, and stuck-run recovery keeps seeing a live turn's tool progress instead of aborting it. Keeping that stream alive does not hand the Gateway an unbounded memory cost: a run that streams hundreds of MiB of message sends retains single-digit MiB of delivery evidence.

## Evidence

