## What Problem This Solves

Visible native children selected the configured subagent model but bypassed the configured subagent-thinking resolver that hidden native children already use. The child could therefore run with an unpersisted or default thinking selection while session surfaces showed the selected model next to an unrelated thinking level and runtime — a child configured as Luna + Codex + Max displayed as the selected model with thinking `Off`.

## Why This Change Was Made

The visible `sessions_spawn` path resolves configured thinking through the existing `resolveSubagentThinkingOverride` owner and passes that level into `sessions.create` beside the already-resolved model. Both configured (saved subagent/agent default) and caller-inherited thinking are clamped through the selected child model/runtime policy before creation, so a saved level the child cannot honor is normalized instead of failing creation. Visible spawns reject per-call `thinking`, so no explicit request is ever downgraded. Auth-profile suffixes are stripped only for that policy lookup, leaving the qualified model ref intact for Gateway creation.

The clamp is graded against the child's authoritative capabilities rather than against logical catalog entries: `sessions.create` **rejects** an explicit `thinkingLevel` its prepared catalog does not support and clamps silently only when the field is absent, so the clamp reads the same prepared-catalog generation the Gateway validates against — selecting the concrete runtime row the way Gateway does (`findModelCatalogEntry` → `selectModelCatalogRuntimeEntry`), with the runtime id resolved once through `resolveEffectiveAgentRuntime` and shared by row selection and level grading. Reasoning, however, is a *logical-model* policy rather than a runtime capability: Gateway validation grades `logical.configuredReasoning ?? logical.reasoning` on top of the selected row, so the clamp forwards that same policy (`resolveCandidateThinkingLevel({ configuredReasoning })`) and the two sides cannot disagree in either direction. When the catalog read fails it forwards no explicit level rather than an unauthorized one. An explicit `model` that is a configured alias is resolved through the same target-agent alias resolver the hidden subagent plan uses, so policy grades the canonical child model instead of a bare alias under the default provider.

Provenance travels with the level: the parent's `thinkingExplicit` fact is forwarded as `callerThinkingExplicit`, so an *automatic* parent tier is left to the child's own model default (exactly as hidden spawning already does) and only configured or explicitly selected effort is persisted.

This is deliberately narrower than #137170: no second effective-reasoning field, no transcript-visibility change, no per-call thinking override with `visible=true`, and no new wire contract.

## User Impact

A visible child configured as Luna + Codex + Max is created, executed and displayed consistently as Luna + Codex + Max. An existing saved subagent thinking setting that visible spawns previously ignored is clamped to what the child supports, so it cannot block child creation. A parent whose own effort is just its model's automatic default no longer imposes that tier on a differently-modelled child.

