Supersedes #160262 (auto-closed for inactivity; branch re-merged with current main).

## What Problem This Solves

Running `pnpm build` in a deployed checkout while the Gateway is up leaves the process half-updated. Chunks it already imported keep working; every import it has not reached yet resolves against the freshly written, differently-hashed files in `dist/` and fails — and nothing says so. The window lasts until someone restarts the Gateway.

Observed on a live host on 2026-09-27: `dist/` was rewritten at 10:51 and 10:58; the Gateway (up since 09-24) was not restarted until 13:20. In those ~2.5 hours:

- `Cannot find module .../dist/subagent-registry-<hash>.mjs imported from .../openclaw-tools.requester-yield-<hash>.mjs` — a chunk that no longer exists under that name.
- Three subagents with `completionTarget=parent` finished and their completions were never delivered.
- A one-shot run completed its work, then died with `The requested module '@openclaw/fs-safe/advanced' does not provide an export named 'copyFileDescriptorSync'` — an already-loaded chunk linking against a package whose exports had moved. Its final reply was never delivered.

Each of those reads as an unrelated bug. Two facts that explain all three already existed and were never put side by side: `hello.server.buildId`, the build the running process loaded (resolved once at module load in `src/version.ts`, so it cannot change without a restart, and already lifted into the probe summary by `src/gateway/probe.ts`), and `dist/build-info.json`, the build currently on disk, which a rebuild replaces underneath the process.

## Why This Change Was Made

Report both and name their divergence. Scoped to that: this does not change how `pnpm build` writes `dist/`, does not stage-and-swap the build directory, and does not retain superseded chunks. It layers onto the build-identity exposure already on `main` at the same call site rather than adding a second one — `DaemonStatus` derives `rpc.server` structurally from the probe result, so `buildId` already flows to the CLI without a type change.

- `src/daemon/service-layout.ts` — `summarizeGatewayServiceLayout` also reports `packageBuildId`, read from the installed service's `dist/build-info.json` at inspection time, bounded to 96 characters to match the protocol bound on `hello.server.buildId`, so a truncated or partially written build-info yields no build id rather than a bogus one status would then compare.
- `src/cli/daemon-cli/status.gateway-identity.ts` (new) — folds the probed version, loaded build id, installed build id and `restartRequired` into one report. Extracted because `status.gather.ts` sits at its 700-line `max-lines` budget.
- `src/cli/daemon-cli/status.gather.ts` — wires the report into `status.gateway`. The on-disk side is supplied **only when the registered native service is the thing that answered**: not for a remote or `--url` probe, not for an explicit `--port`, and (since `65eabfc22e0`) not when that service is unloaded or stopped, or when its runtime PID is not among the probed port's listeners. A foreground Gateway running beside an installed service is explicitly supported by the lifecycle contract, so in those cases no comparison is made and no divergence is claimed; the service's own build id stays readable under `service.layout`.
- `src/cli/daemon-cli/status.print.versions.ts` — prints `Gateway build: <loaded>[, on disk <installed>]`, and on divergence a `Restart required:` warning naming both builds, why imports will fail, and the restart command rendered through `formatCliCommand` so it carries this process's profile and container selection (`openclaw --profile rescue gateway restart`, not a bare command that would restart the default profile's Gateway if pasted elsewhere).
- `src/gateway/stale-install.ts` — `classifyGatewayStaleInstall` also attributes the export-mismatch class, and both classes now carry the build identities in `details` (`runningBuildId`, `onDiskBuildId`) and name both builds in the message.

## User Impact

`openclaw gateway status` answers "is this process running the code on disk?" directly, and when it is not, says so with both build ids and the exact restart command — instead of leaving three unrelated-looking failures to be traced by hand. A foreground Gateway standing beside an installed-but-stopped service is not told to restart for a build it never loaded.

