## What Problem This Solves

The Gateway test harness replaces `agent-model-discovery` with a private `MockModelRegistry` stand-in (`src/gateway/test-helpers.mocks.ts`) that exposed only `getAll`, `getAvailable` and `find`. The prepared model runtime forks its lifecycle-owned template registry when it builds per-run stores — `templateModelRegistry.fork(authStorage, publishedModels)` in `src/agents/prepared-model-runtime.full-catalog.ts` — and `resolveExplicitModelWithRegistry` then reads `getProviderMetadataOwners()` off that fork.

So any Gateway test that reaches the real `prepareModelChoice` / `resolveModelAsync` path with the stand-in installed dies with `TypeError: templateModelRegistry.fork is not a function` before it can exercise the code under test. Production is unaffected: real discovery returns a real `ModelRegistry`, which has both members.

## Why This Change Was Made

The stand-in had fallen behind the contract its production consumer requires. Extending the existing adapter is the smallest complete repair — it keeps the one test-ownership boundary instead of adding a second registry owner, and it keeps harness routing intact: the fork is itself a stand-in, so `agentDiscoveryMock` still controls discovery results, while with the harness disabled it delegates to the wrapped real registry's `fork`. `getProviderMetadataOwners()` delegates the same way.

**This PR changes test infrastructure only.** No production source file is modified; there is no runtime behavior change and no user-facing change.

## User Impact

None at runtime. It unblocks Gateway tests that need the real model-choice path, which otherwise fail on harness plumbing rather than on the behavior they assert.

