## What Problem This Solves

A gate that passes prints nothing terminal. `scripts/lib/tsx-cli-shim.mjs` writes `[tool] FAILED (exit N)` only when the exit code is non-zero, and `runWithFailedTrailer` in `scripts/lib/failed-trailer.mts` does the same, so the stderr of a green `tsgo`, `oxlint`, `check:changed` or ratchet run ends wherever the tool's own output happened to end — and for a quiet tool that is nowhere at all.

That makes a detached or truncated gate log undecidable. A run that passed and a run that was killed before it could write its failure line (OOM kill, runtime cap, host restart, CI step timeout) leave byte-identical logs: no `FAILED` line in either. Whoever reads the log — a person tailing a background lane, or an agent polling one — has to establish the outcome out-of-band (process table, artifact mtimes, re-running the gate), or guesses. A killed run read as "no failure line, so it passed" is the expensive direction.

## Why This Change Was Made

Write one fixed-shape line, `[tool] EXIT <code>`, at the end of every gate run, pass or fail. A killed run leaves no marker, so the marker's presence — not the log's length or the absence of `FAILED` — says the run reached an end.

- `scripts/lib/tsx-cli-shim.mjs` — a wrapper that names a `failureTool` (`tsgo`, `oxlint`, `test`, `tsgo:core:test`, `line-cap-ratchet`, `dup:check`, …) now writes `[tool] EXIT <code>` on every exit path, including the signal path (`EXIT 143`), followed by the existing `FAILED` line when the code is non-zero.
- `scripts/lib/failed-trailer.mts` — `runWithFailedTrailer` (used by `check:changed`, `lint`, `stylelint`, the oxlint shard runner and other implementations that own their trailer) does the same through a new `writeExitTrailer`. A string `process.exitCode` is normalized instead of silently dropping the trailer.
- **The marker reports the run's settled code, not the child's.** An invocation owner whose cleanup can still fail the run opts into deferral with `deferExitTrailer: true` and calls the returned `reportExit()` once that cleanup settles. `scripts/run-vitest.mjs` awaits `workerRun.dispose()` after the shim returns, and disposal awaits `releaseCleanup(…, true)`, which can reject: without deferral a green test child followed by a cleanup-release failure printed `[test] EXIT 0` for a run that exits non-zero, recreating exactly the false success this change exists to remove. The wrapper now reports after disposal and treats a disposal failure as its own gate failure (exit 2), so the marker and the exit code agree. A re-raised signal still writes immediately — the process dies before control returns to the owner — and a process-exit fallback covers an owner that never reports.

Scope is deliberately limited to tools that already own a trailer line. A shimmed wrapper that names no `failureTool` keeps its stderr byte-for-byte unchanged: the product launchers (`run-node`, `watch-node`), stream filters such as `crabbox-remote-redact`, and planners such as `changed-lanes` are not gates and their stderr is not ours to append to. The `FAILED` line stays last, so existing "final line names the failure" contracts hold. Both lines go to stderr; stdout of machine-read tools is untouched.

## User Impact

Contributors and automation reading a gate log can tell "passed" from "failed" from "never finished" using the log alone: `EXIT 0` is a pass, `EXIT N` is a failure, no `EXIT` line means the run did not end. A gate whose cleanup fails after a green child is reported as the failure it is. No change for anything that is not a gate.

