## What Problem This Solves

The singleton CLI MCP loopback listener used to inherit the `AsyncWorkScope` of whichever turn happened to start it. Once that starter turn completed and its scope drained, later plugin callbacks arriving over the same listener — from a different turn, in the same process — were rejected against a closed scope, so multi-turn plugin execution failed with a closed-async-scope error that named nothing useful.

**Most of that is now fixed on `main`, and this PR is scoped to what is left.** `src/gateway/mcp-http.ts` on `main` constructs the listener's own `new GatewayConnectionWork()` and runs the listener inside it (`work.run(() => startMcpLoopbackServer(work))`), so the listener no longer borrows a turn's scope. What main still lacks is the shutdown half:

`startMcpLoopbackServer`'s close path calls `cleanup()` to stop admitting new child grants, then `await`s `httpServer.close(...)` before anything cancels in-flight work. The socket layer decides when that resolves, so request work already in flight keeps running — and keeps holding plugin resources — for as long as the socket drain takes, with no cancellation signal. A runtime being replaced therefore cannot promptly cancel work it has already stopped admitting.

A second gap sits behind the first. Even once shutdown signals the listener's work scope, a tool callback only ever received the HTTP request's own abort signal. A plugin callback that waits on its execute signal while the client stays connected therefore never hears about shutdown, keeps the response open, and `httpServer.close()` waits on it indefinitely.

## Why This Change Was Made

Two small changes in `src/gateway/mcp-http.ts`.

Signal cancellation at the start of shutdown instead of at the end of socket draining:

```ts
cleanup();
// Abort in-flight request work as soon as shutdown starts; do not wait on
// the socket layer to finish draining before signaling cancellation.
work.beginClose();
```

`cleanup()` stays first, so a delayed old-server close cannot revoke a successor runtime's grants. The existing `work.drain()` join is unchanged, so cleanup tails are still awaited.

Hand each tool callback a signal that combines the request's abort with the listener's work scope:

```ts
runWithTrackedCancellation(
  AbortSignal.any([requestAbort.signal, work.signal]),
  handleRequest,
)
```

This is the review's P2 finding, fixed in `2882540ac96`: runtime shutdown now reaches a cooperating tool while it still holds the response open.

## User Impact

A loopback runtime that is shutting down cancels its in-flight tool callbacks immediately, their cleanup still completes, and shutdown no longer hangs on a callback that is waiting for a signal it would never have received. Plugin resources are released promptly when a runtime is replaced.

