## Summary

- **Base branch:** `master`
- **What changed and why:**
  - **The gap.** A browser can't reach a daemon's web dashboard through ZeroRelay today. The relay forwards only end-to-end mTLS (zerocode) and, with `[frontdoor]`, relay-terminated enrollment. #10315 kept "dashboard hosting / a browser session tier" open, and ruled out TLS in page JavaScript.
  - **The tier.** This adds that tier as a disclosed, relay-terminated route, opt-in on both ends. The relay serves an allowlisted daemon's dashboard at `https://<label>.<domain>/`, ending the browser's TLS with the operator's wildcard certificate. People sign in to it as themselves (dashboard sign-in, #11965), each with their own grants. Sign-in is the only way in on this route: pairing, paired tokens, the webhook secret and OIDC enrollment are refused there, because the relay would see the code, token or secret.
  - **Never loopback.** The daemon hands those connections to its gateway in process, under the browser's address. Dialling the gateway's port would have made every relay browser a loopback peer, with the admin routes, the limiter exemptions and SOP cancellation that come with it.
  - **The address.** Each dashboard is its own origin, so nodes can't read each other's cookies or storage. The label comes from the daemon's relay registration key: it survives node-id rotation, only that daemon can be served under it, and the node-id stays out of URLs, DNS and TLS server names.
- **Scope boundary:** this does not change:
  - the RPC plane or zerocode/native enrollment, which stay blind;
  - the frontdoor enrollment page;
  - direct gateway deployments: their scheme, callback and origin rules are unchanged, and #12048's configured origin for them stays its own change.

  It adds no TLS in JavaScript, no path-prefix addressing, no HTTP/2 on this route, and no proxying of the identity provider or any other local port.
- **Blast radius:**
  - **gateway:** a second source of connections in the accept loop, and the scheme, callback and own-origin helpers now take a marker. Relayed requests that present any credential but the session cookie are refused, as are relayed requests to the pairing and enrollment routes;
  - **runtime:** the relay bridge's `Open` handling;
  - **zerorelay:** the accept path, after the TLS handshake;
  - **protocol:** three optional fields;
  - **config:** a new `[relay.dashboard]`, which requires `[gateway.dashboard_sign_in]` with a provider;
  - **web:** the sign-in screen (sign-in only, through a relay; no pairing form until the gateway says how to sign in) and shell.
- **Linked issue(s):** Related #10315 (phase 2 of its item 1). Related #12048: this folds in the part relay requests need, a fixed origin and 421 on a mismatched `Host`; the `public_url` setting for direct deployments stays there. Follow-up: #12285, refusing relayed config writes to credential and sign-in paths.

### What this does, simply

Today, reaching your agent's web dashboard from outside your network needs a VPN, an open port, or a tunnel. With this, someone running a ZeroRelay can give your daemon a dashboard address of its own, such as `https://abcd....relay.example.com/`. You turn it on in your daemon's config, and the relay operator lists your daemon. Anyone you've given an account signs in there with your identity provider and gets only what their account allows. Pairing codes and paired tokens don't work at that address, because the relay would see them.

The trade-off is stated everywhere it applies. The relay decrypts this traffic to forward it, so whoever runs the relay could read or change those dashboard sessions. The daemon and relay both warn at startup, the docs explain it, and the sign-in screen says so while you're connected this way. Native clients (zerocode) through the same relay stay end-to-end, and are the option when the relay must not be trusted. Even a hostile relay doesn't get identity-provider passwords. It isn't handed a pairing code or a lasting token to keep, can't reach anything on the daemon but the dashboard, can't make a browser count as the daemon's own machine, and can't exceed the signed-in user's grants. It can do whatever the signed-in user can, though, so an administrator signed in through a relay hands it administrator power. Administer the daemon directly or with zerocode.

### Review history

- **Round 1** (automated review): the dashboard route now opens on the registration its label matched (4003605bff). A browser that stops reading is let go after the idle timeout (e5f6d12920). A paired browser keeps the relay notice after a reload (08d75fa060). The runtime placement exception is recorded separately in #12274, which must land first.
- **Round 2** (automated review): the dashboard's closes are bounded for a browser that never reads (7e56cf4471).
